## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical ability to apply basic AWS guardrails in a real-world multi-team setup.

## Key Concepts

- IAM least privilege with per-prefix access
- S3 bucket policy enforcement for encryption and public access
- Event-driven validation with Lambda on PutObject
- Observability via CloudWatch

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal Lambda skeleton to validate encryption and prefix
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const r = event.Records[0];
  const bucket = r.s3.bucket.name;
  const key = r.s3.object.key;
  const enc = r.s3.object.serverSideEncryption;
  if (!enc) throw new Error("Object not encrypted");
  if (!/^corp-logs\/team-(a|b|c)\//.test(key)) throw new Error("Wrong prefix");
  return;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this pattern for more teams?
- How would you test revocation and auditing of access?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-account temporary access, least-privilege IAM policies, and time-bound credentials using STS.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account trust via AssumeRole
- Least privilege scoped to a specific S3 prefix
- Role duration controls expiry
- Testing access during and after the window

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy (for the role in OUR account)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::PARTNER_ACCOUNT_ID:root"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Role policy (least privilege for PIX123)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::shared-docs",
      "Condition": {"StringLike": {"s3:prefix": ["contracts/PIX123/*"]}}
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::shared-docs/contracts/PIX123/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you revoke access before expiry if required?
- How would you audit the access and ensure logs show the temporary session usage?

Why This Is Asked

This question probes tenancy boundaries across AWS accounts and how to enforce data residency and least-privilege access in a scalable, automated way.

Key Concepts

- Cross-account AssumeRole and STS
- Per-tenant S3 + CMK in tenant accounts
- Least-privilege IAM roles and bucket policies
- Data residency, rotation, and auditability
- DR/backup and cross-region considerations

Code Example

